From ca3ec5a17c3de8a9a0bc38ef87e5b1069fad533d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colum <git@volf.co>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 10:25:36 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] dsv41: TF_DS_ROUND_PROFILE (off by default) gives the
 served process the round profile the batch driver prints, at /metrics

Switch: TF_DS_ROUND_PROFILE (1 or 2; off otherwise). Read once at open by rank 0's served process (the dsv41 CUDA family's open, before the Loaded is built): exactly `1` sets the same counters the batch driver's --profile sets (Lanes.prof, Model.prof, the Engram host profile's eh.profile), exactly `2` also times the rounds' phases (Model.usePhaseTimer, eager rounds). Anything else (unset, `0`, `off`, `true`) sets nothing, allocates nothing and prints nothing: the replies are bit for bit the base ones and /metrics is untouched. TF_DS_CLOCK_MARKS is read as the batch driver reads it (u8, default 0, trimmed) and Model.useClock is called with it; marks 0 is exactly a batch driver with no TF_DS_CLOCK_MARKS (the forward's start and each stretch's ends only).

The profile is attributed to a scrape, not a run, and the numbers must not be read as the unprofiled round cost. The profile syncs the GPU every round (with Model.prof on, verify synchronizes before and after the forward and the Engram host's stamps synchronize), so the figures include the sync. It is printed at /metrics, the one place a live pair can be read without stopping it, and the caveat rides with the numbers in the log: a separate line `{"rank": 0, "profile_note": "attribution only: the round profile syncs the GPU every round; figures include the sync and are not the unprofiled round cost"}` prints last, after the profile and clock lines. The profile line itself stays exactly the batch driver's shape, so it survives a strict key-equality compare against `tf-dsv41-lanes --profile`; the note is its own line, not a key on the profile line. The README row (added separately) carries the same attribution-only caveat, as does the line.

/metrics and not every N rounds: the lane core (core/lanes/engine.zig) has no hook a family could use for a periodic print, and the profile is a diagnostics switch for a live pair, not a steady-state feature; the counters are cumulative since process start and are never reset, and the `rounds` counter in the line lets a reader take deltas between scrapes. The values may be torn mid-round (u64 loads, no snapshot lock); the next scrape fixes them. The print fires once per /metrics scrape, after the reply is sent (conn.finish), via a family-agnostic Engine.roundProfile hook: a new optional vtable method (null for Metal and the other families, which leave LaneHost.round_profile null and print nothing) and a one-line api.Engine wrapper. Nothing prints on the request path, nothing prints when the switch is off (one null check and an indirect call, no allocation), and the Prometheus body in status_routes is byte for byte what it was (metrics.zig is untouched).

The wiring mirrors kv_parts/engram. LaneHost gains round_profile (null by default); its vtable exposes roundProfileFn, which calls the family's print when set. The dsv41 open sets it (Loaded.round_profile, the CUDA host forwards it to the lane host, both with @hasField on the family's Loaded type), and status_routes' metrics() calls srv.engine.roundProfile() after the reply. Metal, glm_host and qwen35 construct their LaneHosts unchanged and leave round_profile null: they print nothing at /metrics.

printRoundProfile (dsv41/native.zig) ports the batch driver's end-of-process print to std.log.info (stderr, the container's log; one JSON object per line, only the JSON object as this code's text):
1. The lanes_main Profile line, verbatim in shape: {"rank": 0, "profile": {"rounds": .., "rows_a_round": .., "step_ms": -1, "send_ms": .., "enqueue_ms": .., "forward_ms": .., "absorb_ms": .., "logits_ms": .., "sample_ms": .., "passes": .., "streams_a_pass": .., "pass_ms": .., "prefills": .., "prefill_ms": ..}}, computed with the batch driver's own arithmetic (rows_a_round and the others from the same nanoseconds-per-round/pass/prefill ratios and formats, the if (n == 0) 0 guard where a count is 0).
2. With clk_rounds > 0, the two printClock lines (round_clock_us, round_launches), ported with rank the literal 0, m.rd.launches, round.PhaseTimer.phases and round.Round.clock_wait/clock_tail.
3. With m.timer != null (switch 2), the phases_ms line; no "chunks": that is prefill_phases_ms in the batch driver, which this path does not print.
4. The profile_note line above, always.

step_ms is -1: the batch driver gets it from a local step's ns over core.steps, which the serve path does not have, so -1 is a sentinel for "not measured (serve path)" and every other field is real.

Host gates (this host): `zig build test` exits 0 (its runner reported one spurious "failed command" for fedec0d2cb3b809f6ce4dfea3c223076/test on the first run of the suite, exactly the flaky big binaries the untouched branch shows; that binary passes standalone). The standalone gate (every .zig-cache/o subdirectory's test binary run directly) passes all 14 binaries. `zig build install native -Dfatbins=/home/colum/.cache/tf-dummy-fatbins` succeeds (the CUDA path type-checks; no CUDA code changed). Not run here, host-only: any pair, any smoke, exact.py. The bench pair should: start with TF_DS_ROUND_PROFILE=1 (plus TF_DS_CLOCK_MARKS to taste), scrape /metrics under load, and compare the profile line against `tf-dsv41-lanes --profile` (strict key-equality holds; step_ms is -1 by design). exact.py must stay IDENTICAL with the switch on: the profile syncs the GPU, it does not change a token. With the switch unset, /metrics' output is unchanged.

